import{_ as i,P as a,d as n,e as s,y as o,q as r,f as l}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const g=JSON.parse(`{"title":"D.10 Ethical Disagreement, Capabilities, and Bounded Responsibility","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.10 Ethical Disagreement, Capabilities, and Bounded Responsibility","xkChars":0,"xkWords":596,"xkLessons":{"D.10":{"title":"D.10 Ethical Disagreement, Capabilities, and Bounded Responsibility","lead":"Even if all parties agree on the benefits an arrangement would produce and who would be harmed, they may still disagree about whether it is permissible. Making the normative premises of disagreement public is more honest than inventing a future of inevitable failure. Resources, opportunities, choices, life outcomes, and responsibility must also remain separate.","explanation":["Chinese source location: lines 5124–5141; source anchor section-d-10; brief quotation translated from Chinese: “The same facts can leave genuine normative disagreement; there is no need to fabricate empirical effects to eliminate it.” An exact source excerpt is separately attached in source-slice.md; explanation and source text remain separate.","Virtue ethics concerns practical judgment and how actors live; deontology emphasizes constraints such as not treating people merely as means; consequentialism compares benefits and harms. These are rough orientations used to introduce questions here, not complete definitions or claims that each tradition has no internal disagreements. Analysis should place different reasons within the same specific question, stating what each permits and prohibits rather than first declaring one tradition universally victorious.","Empirical facts can constrain normative arguments without necessarily resolving disagreement alone. Both sides may acknowledge an arrangement's higher total benefit, while one still considers it a violation of independent requirements of basic standing. Opposition cannot be supported by inventing a claim that the arrangement must eventually collapse economically; nor does short-term efficiency establish justification. Normative restrictions may be advanced independently, with their reasons and applicability boundaries explained.","Conversion conditions connect resources, achievable capabilities, actual choices, and life outcomes. The same money need not create identical feasible opportunities for people with different care responsibilities, disability support, transportation, and institutional conditions. Identical outcomes may also arise from very different ranges of choice. Comparison should ask what genuine options people possess, rather than counting only inputs or final activities; not actually choosing something also does not establish an absence of capability.","Multiple individual maximum capabilities may not be jointly achievable. Given one day's time, for example, it may be used for learning or care, with high maxima for each separately, but these maxima cannot be combined into a life attainable within the same day. Preserve feasible combinations and mutual constraints. A capability table listing many possible activities does not establish that they can all be completed together, still less require the person to realize them all.","Willingness to accept, affordability, and having no alternatives are different states. Accepting an arrangement under urgent need may show only that it is feasible relative to available alternatives, not that burdens are light, distribution fair, or conditions of free choice complete. Examine alternatives, risks, time, and actual room to refuse; the fact of acceptance cannot automatically end further normative discussion.","Adding interpersonal utilities requires explaining comparable units and weights. Being able to write a weighted sum does not automatically determine whose unit equals whose, or which losses may be offset. Numerical representation expresses choice premises; it does not generate them. Sensitivity analysis can show how conclusions change with weights, but selected weights must not be hidden as the only objective scale.","Potential compensation and actual compensation especially must not be confused. Total benefits exceeding total losses establishes only that a compensating arrangement may exist under particular assumptions about transferable resources. Without actual payment, appropriate receipt, or if the harm cannot be fully replaced, harmed people continue bearing loss. Beneficiaries being able in principle to afford compensation is not completed compensation, and a monetary net gain does not establish that every loss in life can be offset.","Freedom may be understood in terms of present interference, or in terms of subjection to arbitrary power. The latter non-domination perspective is not a fact automatically read from a physical state; it is a normative choice requiring public defense. Under this perspective, someone with power to interfere arbitrarily choosing not to do so for now differs from the absence of that arbitrary-control relation. Analysis must state its evaluative criteria, not conflate them into one self-evident universal quantity of freedom.","Cultural differences do not automatically cancel shared protections, while protective claims must also address lived practices and implementation costs. Neither side can end discussion with a label: “this is tradition” cannot justify ignoring harmed people, and declaring universal principles cannot replace examining concrete implementation conditions, the voices of those involved, and risks. Protective values, modes of implementation, and empirical effects each have argumentative responsibilities.","Responsibility should be separated into attribution of conduct, fault, causal contribution, benefit, capacity to prevent, and remedy. An institution's ability to help does not impose unlimited responsibility; absence of fault does not automatically exclude relief arrangements grounded in independent reasons. Responsibility items may hold in different directions; one overall responsibility label cannot settle capability, facts, and duties at once. This framework does not decide specific legal liability.","Tragic choices may have no lossless option. Even when bounded procedural duties have been fulfilled, irreversible losses in people's lives may remain. Record which claims were unmet, which harms cannot be repaired, and who subsequently bears what on which grounds. Deleting losses from indicators does not make a choice more responsible. The eight entries are, in order: three major ethical traditions; utility and commensurability; Rawls and debates on justice; Sen's and Nussbaum's capability approaches; freedom, non-domination, and community; human rights, culture, and universality; responsibility, remedy, intergenerational and ecological justice; and dilemmas, tragedy, and incomparability. This lesson does not pretend a table of contents has resolved those internal debates.","The care example added in v2 makes tragic choice concrete in positions and responsibilities: one night-shift care worker faces two residents needing assistance simultaneously, with delay creating irreversible risk for either. If a rule prioritizing more imminent danger recommends helping one first, the other's legitimate claim, loss, and the institution's remedial responsibility must remain, along with the question of who decided to staff the night with only one person.","This example declares no ethical principle eternally correct, nor does its limited information establish dereliction by the care worker. Under §14.9.1, check actual constraints, whether options have been omitted, escalation channels when authority is insufficient, and non-waivable protections. Analytical recommendation and real-world emergency authority each require their own grounds."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Factual disagreement","leftText":"Disagreement about what happens and the magnitude of consequences.","rightTitle":"Normative disagreement","rightText":"Even with agreed facts, permissible scope and constraints on basic standing may differ."},{"leftTitle":"Possessing resources","leftText":"Money, goods, or service inputs.","rightTitle":"Genuinely feasible capabilities","rightText":"Also depend on care, transportation, bodily support, and institutional conversion conditions."},{"leftTitle":"Individual maximum","leftText":"The maximum attainable on each dimension separately.","rightTitle":"Jointly feasible combinations","rightText":"Multiple maxima may not be simultaneously achievable; constraints must remain."},{"leftTitle":"Potentially compensable","leftText":"Enough resources exist under assumptions to design compensation.","rightTitle":"Already compensated","rightText":"Actual transfer and uptake of losses require checking, and some losses are irreplaceable."},{"leftTitle":"Bounded duties fulfilled","leftText":"Certain procedures or actions have been completed under the stated norms.","rightTitle":"Life losses disappear","rightText":"Irreversible losses may remain; complete repair cannot automatically be declared."}],"boundaries":["Unsupported claims of future inefficiency must not be used to prove that every unjust arrangement must fail.","Utility aggregation must disclose interpersonal units, weights, and non-offsettable constraints; mathematical representation does not authorize itself.","Capability creates no unlimited duty; absence of fault does not automatically exclude relief based on independent reasons.","This lesson does not determine legal liability, eligibility, or compulsory compensation in actual cases.","All numbers below are teaching constructions, not estimates of social welfare or prices assigned to particular people's worth."],"questions":["Why can disagreement remain after agreement on every fact?","Why might two individual maximum capabilities not be achievable together?","Does absence of fault necessarily mean no reason for relief exists?","Why can a weighted sum not determine the weights?"],"diagramPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/figures/section-d-10.png","caseLinks":[{"title":"An actual official historical account: shared protective claims remain separate from realization","href":"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"An actual official historical account: shared protective claims remain separate from realization","text":"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights page of OHCHR's Regional Office for Europe states that representatives from different legal and cultural backgrounds drafted the document and advanced universal protection of fundamental human rights. “The original records” paragraph states that the UN General Assembly adopted it in Paris on 10 December 1948. This confirms a public shared protective claim and its adoption, but adoption cannot be equated with realization everywhere, nor can the page adjudicate any specific conflict of rights.","sourceLabel":"Official introduction from OHCHR's Regional Office for Europe","sourceUrl":"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights","sourceLocator":"Introductory paragraph after the main heading (line 59); first paragraph of The original records (line 74)","boundary":"Only the official historical introduction was checked. No claim is made to have read all original meeting records, the resolution text, or implementation material from every country. Contemporary legal force is not interpreted, and no case-specific legal advice is given.","href":"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"D.10","start_line":5180,"end_line":5197,"startLine":5180,"endLine":5197}],"outputId":"section-d-10","sourceHash":"fcf100a5c8688d1e0337c52750ce3ad21fb5dbcc250f97946602ce094345f825","argumentSteps":["Fix the same concrete factual situation and separately disclose normative reasons concerning virtues, duties, consequences, and other considerations.","Distinguish resources, capabilities, choices, and life outcomes, checking conversion conditions and genuinely feasible combinations.","Disclose interpersonal scales, weights, and limits on offsetting; record potential compensation and actual uptake separately.","Separate attribution of conduct, fault, causality, benefit, prevention, and remedy, stating material and independent normative reasons for each.","Record unmet claims and irreversible losses where no lossless option exists; do not resolve disagreement through fabricated inefficiency or deleted indicators."],"teachingExamples":[{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: individual maxima cannot be combined into a life","text":"Suppose 10 hours are available in a day, with learning x and care y satisfying x≥0, y≥0, and x+y≤10. Maximizing x alone gives 10, and maximizing y alone also gives 10, but (10,10) requires 20 hours and is infeasible. The joint capability set must preserve the triangular constraint; two marginal maxima cannot construct a nonexistent combination.","assumptions":"Time is continuously divisible and other activities are ignored: a simple finite-resource model.","boundary":"This does not prescribe how a real person should allocate a day, nor imply that care and learning can always be separated this way."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: a net gain is not completed compensation","text":"Suppose an arrangement gives A 100 units of transferable resources and costs B 60 units, a net gain of 40. If A actually transfers 0 to B, B still loses 60; if A transfers 60, A retains 40 and B's resource loss becomes zero. The latter conclusion additionally relies on the strong assumption that all relevant losses can be fully compensated by the same kind of resources, which real life may not satisfy.","assumptions":"Monetary units are purely constructed; enforceability and transferability of compensation are additional assumptions.","boundary":"A potential transfer is not actual payment, still less a price for irreversible harm."},{"title":"Mathematical teaching construction: the same facts, different normative permissions","text":"Suppose arrangement A gives two people resource outcomes (12,0), B gives (5,5), and some norm establishes a minimum of 4 for each person as a non-offsettable floor. Maximizing only the equally weighted total selects A's 12 over B's 10; adding the floor makes A impermissible and B feasible. The conclusions arise from public normative differences, without changing resource facts or inventing A's future collapse.","assumptions":"This illustrates only the difference between aggregate and floor rules; both numbers and the floor are specified by the author.","boundary":"It does not establish 4 as the correct real-world floor, nor treat resources as a complete proxy for all welfare or basic standing."},{"title":"Teaching construction: grounds for relief need not be fault","text":"Imagine an institution did not cause an accident but takes up bounded assistance under an explicitly and voluntarily established relief arrangement. Its independent reasons, resources, and boundaries can be discussed without inventing fault to prove relief is possible or demanding unlimited burdens merely because capacity exists.","assumptions":"A fictional institution and arrangement, with no actual commitments or funds.","boundary":"Specific duties require corresponding norms and authorization; the example does not decide legal liability."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"The arrangement is deeply unjust, so it must soon collapse through inefficiency.","effect":"Normative opposition can have independent reasons; future effects still require empirical grounds.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"The benefit of 100 exceeds the loss of 60, so the harmed person has already been compensated.","effect":"A net gain of 40 does not establish a transfer occurred; actual losses and compensation conditions require separate checks.","sourceIds":["theory"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Procedural duties have been fulfilled, so no irreversible losses remain.","effect":"Fulfillment of duties and losses in life are different judgments; unrecovered losses must be retained honestly.","sourceIds":["theory"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Why can disagreement remain after agreement on every fact?","answer":"Permissible offsetting, constraints on basic standing, weights, and reasons for responsibility may differ; normative premises must be public.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why might two individual maximum capabilities not be achievable together?","answer":"They may compete for the same time resource, so the combination of marginal maxima lies outside the jointly feasible set.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Does absence of fault necessarily mean no reason for relief exists?","answer":"Not necessarily. Relief may have other independent reasons, but boundaries should be disclosed rather than inventing fault or unlimited duties.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"},{"question":"Why can a weighted sum not determine the weights?","answer":"The formula only expresses results under given weights; weights and comparability of interpersonal units require separate justification.","basis":"The source text of this section and the public derivation above"}],"figures":[{"path":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/figures/section-d-10.png","alt":"Keep facts consistent and normative reasons public, without inventing inevitable inefficiency; resources become capabilities conditionally, and individual limits do not establish joint feasibility; weighted sums require public units/weights and boundaries on offsetting; potential compensation is not actual compensation, and irreversible losses remain; attribution/fault/causality and benefit/prevention/remedy determine bounded responsibilities separately; a lossless option may not exist, and fulfilling procedural duties does not erase losses in people's lives. Drawn by the author based on D.10; numbers and normative floors are teaching constructions, not actual legal or welfare judgments.","caption":"Drawn by the author based on D.10; numbers and normative floors are teaching constructions, not actual legal or welfare judgments."}],"diagramBrief":"The current deliverable is a static diagram; interaction has not been implemented. References below to selection, switching, or clicking are only future design ideas. Switch aggregate rules versus independent floors, whether actual compensation occurred, and whether capability combinations are feasible to show where disagreements lie.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"An official historical account illustrates shared normative claims and the event of adoption; the existence of norms does not replace implementation effects or permission for a real-world case."},"openQuestions":["The original research for all entries 10.01–10.08 was not obtained; a table of contents cannot resolve each tradition's internal disagreements on its behalf.","This case checked only the official historical account. The original resolution and meeting records were not successfully obtained, and implementation in different places or contemporary legal force was not reviewed."],"taskId":"S-D-10","sources":[{"id":"theory","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, source text supplied by the user","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68","locator":"Historical Chinese research manuscript, full-text lines 4673–4688; see sourceRefs for each section's exact range","claim_support":["D.10: terminology, contract, argumentative responsibilities, and source boundaries; not a basis for certifying empirical facts"],"limits":["The author's theoretical text is the object being explained, not a substitute for external empirical material. Skill version 5.3.1 is not the theory's version.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2.1.1."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"ohchr-udhr-history","title":"Universal Declaration of Human Rights — OHCHR Europe","url":"https://europe.ohchr.org/universal-declaration-human-rights","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"The page has no publication date; the historical event described occurred on 1948-12-10","locator":"Introductory paragraph after the main heading (line 59); first paragraph of The original records (line 74)","claim_support":["Drafting by representatives from different legal and cultural backgrounds and the claim of universal protection; adopted by the General Assembly in Paris on 1948-12-10"],"limits":["The original resolution and full meeting record were not obtained; no inference is made about contemporary legal force, implementation in different places, or answers to particular normative conflicts."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2.1","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2.1, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2.1 / 2026-11-19","locator":"D.10　伦理分歧、能力与有限责任; Chinese source lines 5124–5141; SHA-256 2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2.1; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/source-slice.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-D-10/diagrams.md","editionNote":"This page’s source text, section locations and lesson wording correspond to v2.1; historical research attachments and prior external-reading records retain their own versions and scopes.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"e492250a91dc39d9bbf929d63e78d1ba2b321aad1e30938f3fd38af890745b4c","source_sha256":"2128af7cfa4c63b58207521a375aa34c2aea230d47e1c04a5afec3334e290d28"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-10.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-10.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-10.md"};function d(u,e,h,m,p,f){const t=a("SectionLesson");return n(),s("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o("",1)),r(t,{section:"D.10"}),e[1]||(e[1]=l("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(c,[["render",d]]);export{g as __pageData,y as default};
